# Semantic HTML

Prefer native HTML semantics and attributes over CSS class names that restate state.

## Disabled controls

- Put `disabled` on the actual control (`button`, `input`, `select`, `textarea`, `fieldset`, `option`).
- Do **not** use a `.disabled` class to fake disabled state.
- Style disabled controls with `:disabled` or a parent `:has(:disabled)`.
- Elements that cannot take `disabled` (e.g. `div`, `li`, `details`):
  - Interactive widgets → use a real `button` / `input` when possible.
  - Whole subtrees → use the `inert` attribute.
  - Custom options / listbox items → use `aria-disabled="true"` and style `[aria-disabled="true"]`.

```html
<!-- BAD -->
<span class="icon disabled" onclick="...">Delete</span>
<label class="field disabled"><input type="range"></label>

<!-- GOOD -->
<button type="button" disabled>Delete</button>
<label class="field"><input type="range" disabled></label>
```

```scss
// BAD
.field.disabled { opacity: 0.45; }

// GOOD
.field:has(:disabled) { opacity: 0.45; }
button:disabled { opacity: 0.45; }
```

## General

- Use the element that matches the role: `button` for actions, `a` for navigation, `label` + control for form fields.
- Prefer native `open` / `inert` / `hidden` / `disabled` / `readonly` / `required` over class toggles when an HTML attribute exists.
- Keep ARIA as a supplement when no native attribute applies — do not replace a native control with `role` + click handlers.
